ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

NETWORKING

This fork adds a virtio-net driver and a small TCP/IP stack
(kernel/virtio_net.c, net.c, tcp.c, sysnet.c), and a web server
(user/httpd.c).  "make qemu" attaches qemu's user-mode network and
forwards host port 8080 to port 80 in xv6 (change the host port with
"make qemu NETPORT=8081").  In the xv6 shell run

  $ httpd &

and open http://localhost:8080/ on the host.  "/" is user/index.html;
other paths are files on the xv6 file system, e.g. /README.

The stack is server-side TCP only, with no retransmission, and is
meant to stay small and readable rather than complete.  Set NET_DEBUG
to 1 in kernel/net.h to log every frame.  "./test-xv6.py net"
exercises it from the host.
